\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We test the race on linear JEPAs where its assumptions hold (E1, the development set), on held-out settings that break them (E1b), on masking (E2) and on a ViT (E3); E1b--E3 were pre-registered.

\subsection{E1: linear JEPA with aligned statistics (development set)}
\label{sec:e1}

\paragraph{Set-up.}
We take $n=d=\EoneN$, $\sigma_k=1$ and $c_k=0.9^k$, and train the population loss \eqref{eq:loss} by full-batch gradient descent (step $\EoneEta$) with $\Pred_0=p_0I$ and $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$, from an orthogonal encoder of scale $a_0$ (where \cref{prop:reduction} is exact) or a Gaussian one, over grids in $\lambda$, $p_0$, $\alpha$, $a_0$, $\kappa$ and $L$ (\EoneRuns{} runs; \cref{app:details}).
The rank counts singular values of $\Wc$ above \EoneSvThr{} at a horizon $T$ (at convergence in E1b); baselines are the initial window $\#\{k:g_k(p_0)>0\}$ and the static bound $\#\{k:c_k^2\ge4\lambda\sigma_k\}$; brackets: bootstrap 95\% intervals.

\paragraph{Results.}
With orthogonal initialisation the criterion classifies the fate of \EoneModeAccOrth{} of the \EoneModes{} directions and gives the exact rank in \EoneRankExactOrth{} of runs, against \EoneWindowExactOrth{} for the initial window; the window misses by \EoneWindowMaeOrth{} directions on average and the static bound overestimates by \EoneBoundMaeOrth{} in every run (\cref{tab:accuracy}, \cref{fig:e1-rank,fig:e1-traj}).
The knobs move the rank as predicted (\cref{fig:e1-knobs}, \cref{app:details}): without weight decay nothing survives at $p_0=1$, because $p_0\ge c_k/\sigma_k$ for every direction, although the static bound counts all \EoneN; the rank is non-monotone in $\lambda$ and $p_0$ and falls with $\alpha$ (Gaussian initialisation, EMA rate, depth: \cref{app:details}).

